uld make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 6x & =(\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503666 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503666 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503666 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503666 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503666 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f68ddcf-7f49-5e13-8fb1-2db21d4acf5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503666 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{x+3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=x+3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =x+3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2}) & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (\\frac{3x+15}{3}) & =3\\cdot (\\frac{2x+12}{3}) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 5 & =(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
